1.30.m:
Therefore one should always worship him preceded by devotion,
with ear-ointments, pointed sweets, and modakas, O king;
one should continually worship the divine tusked one for the destruction of obstacles. 9

1.34.m:
Especially under Ārdrā, Āśleṣā, Maghā, Bharaṇī, and Kṛttikā;
under Viśākhā, the three Pūrvās, Mūla, Svātī, and Śatabhiṣaj:
those bitten by snakes do not live, nor do those by whom poison has been drunk.

And if a base person is the messenger for a noble person, or if a noble person is the messenger for a base person;
if he speaks the name of the bitten at first, or if he speaks it thereafter;
if the distinction of caste is equal, he is the messenger of one whose time of death has arrived.

Or if the messenger has a staff in hand, or is a pair, or holds a noose in hand;
wears red or black garments, has them over his face or head, or is a messenger wearing a single cloth;
likewise if he is smeared with oil, walks with hurried gait, has dishevelled hair,
and makes a terrible sound with both hands and feet, he is the messenger of one whose time of death has arrived.

1.39.m:
Indeed, he who undertakes a fast with a nocturnal meal becomes an observer of vows through the nocturnal meal;
in this world or the next he obtains the highest renown,
and he obtains a fixed dwelling in heaven, there is no doubt about this.

1.40.m:
Sumaṃtu said:
On this matter, Brahmā was questioned of old by his wise disciples;
what was spoken by him to them, that I speak to you; learn it from me.
